From: Hans Christian Winther-Sorensen <hc@wsh.no>
Subject: [PATCH] SPARC64: name UPA and ebus children like OBP (pci@1f,0,
 su@14,3f8)

The sabre was named after its first reg cell, "pci@1fe,0", and the ebus
children got the PCI unit address format of the bridge methods: "su@0",
"8042@0", "eeprom@0". OBP on an Ultra 5/10 names them "pci@1f,0" (the
UPA port id, reg cell 0 = 1c0 + 2 * port id) and "su@14,3f8" (ebus
space,offset).

Solaris builds those OBP names for its device nodes and looks the
firmware's stdin/stdout paths up among them. With the old names Solaris
10 found no match for "/pci@1fe,0/pci@1,1/ebus@1/su@0", never opened the
su driver as the console, and the installer took no input on ttya: the
characters arrived in the 16550 (LSR data ready), and nothing read them.

Encode the root's unit addresses as port ids (decode accepts both forms)
and give the ebus node a two-cell space,offset encode-unit/decode-unit.
With this the stdin instance path is
/pci@1f,0/pci@1,1/ebus@1/su@14,3f8, and Solaris 10's installer reads
ttya.

paulsheer/openbios 5d7938c2 makes the same two naming changes in its
own way; this is a separate implementation.

maxinjohn/proxmox-solaris (solaris.sh, 2026-11-14) maps the sabre's unit
address between 1f and 1fe the same way for Solaris.
---
 arch/sparc64/tree.fs | 23 ++++++++++++++++++-----
 drivers/pci.c        |  7 +++++++
 2 files changed, 25 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/sparc64/tree.fs b/arch/sparc64/tree.fs
index f390f6c..fc653ac 100644
--- a/arch/sparc64/tree.fs
+++ b/arch/sparc64/tree.fs
@@ -4,17 +4,30 @@ include config.fs
 \ UPA encode/decode unit
 \ -------------------------------------------------------------------------
 
-: decode-unit-upa ( str len -- id lun )
-  2 parse-nhex
-;
-
-: encode-unit-upa ( id lun -- str len)
+\ Two cells as "first,second" in hex
+: encode-unit-2hex ( first second -- str len )
   swap
   pocket tohexstr
   " ," pocket tmpstrcat >r
   rot pocket tohexstr r> tmpstrcat drop
 ;
 
+\ A UPA child's reg starts with 1c0 + 2 * port-id (sabre: 1fe = port 1f).
+\ OBP names it by the port id, "pci@1f,0", and so does Solaris when it
+\ builds the same name; accept both forms.
+: upa-portid>bustype ( n -- bustype )
+  dup 20 < if 1 lshift 1c0 or then
+;
+
+: decode-unit-upa ( str len -- id lun )
+  2 parse-nhex upa-portid>bustype
+;
+
+: encode-unit-upa ( id lun -- str len)
+  1 rshift 1f and
+  encode-unit-2hex
+;
+
 \ ---------
 \ DMA words
 \ ---------
diff --git a/drivers/pci.c b/drivers/pci.c
index f30e427..bdf8e22 100644
--- a/drivers/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/pci.c
@@ -1114,6 +1114,13 @@ int ebus_config_cb(const pci_config_t *config)
 
     set_property(dev, "ranges", (char *)props, ncells * sizeof(props[0]));
 
+    /* Children of the ebus are addressed by their two reg cells,
+       space,offset: "su@14,3f8" as on OBP, not by the PCI unit address
+       format inherited from the bridge methods. Solaris builds that name
+       for the node and matches the console's path against it. */
+    feval(": decode-unit ( str len -- phys.lo phys.hi ) 2 parse-nhex ;");
+    feval(": encode-unit ( phys.lo phys.hi -- str len ) encode-unit-2hex ;");
+
     /*  Build eeprom node */
     fword("new-device");
     PUSH(0x14);
